\subsection{Output format}%
\label{output-format}

Many commands output data in CSV format.
By default we use the tab character as the delimiter, since this gives us 
compatibility with all the POSIX tools available at the command line.
But we provide an option to change the delimiter, \eg to a comma.
<<add global options to argp>>=
argp.add_argument("-d", "--delimiter",
  default="\t",
  help="Sets the delimiter for CSV output, the default is the tab character")
argp.add_argument("-q", "--quiet",
  action="count",
  default=0,
  help="Decrease verbosity: -q=ERROR only, -qq=CRITICAL only, -qqq=silent")
argp.add_argument("-v", "--verbose",
  action="count",
  default=0,
  help="Increase verbosity: -v=INFO, -vv=DEBUG, -vvv=all library debug")
@


\subsection{Cache control}

Most commands speed up by caching the Canvas API object between runs.
Occasionally you want to bypass the cache for a single command,
for example right after creating or updating objects.

<<add global options to argp>>=
argp.add_argument("--no-cache",
  action="store_true",
  help="Do not read or write the persistent Canvas object cache")
@


\subsection{Why global options must stay global}%
\label{sec:global-options-are-global}

Every option above is attached to [[argp]], the top-level parser, and that is
exactly what makes it available to all subcommands.
The reach comes with an obligation on the subcommand modules: no subparser may
declare an option that shares a [[dest]] with a global one.

The reason lies in how argparse hands control over to a subparser.
When [[argp.parse_args]] reaches the subcommand name, it does not keep filling
in the namespace it has built so far.
It lets the subparser parse the remaining words into a \emph{fresh} namespace,
then copies every attribute of that namespace onto the outer one.
That copy is unconditional: it also covers options the user never wrote, whose
values are nothing but the subparser's defaults.
A duplicated option therefore does not merely shadow the global one---its
default silently overwrites what the global option had already computed.

The symptom is quiet, and it points away from the cause.
Consider a subcommand that declares a [[-v]] of its own with
[[default=False]]:
\begin{center}
[[canvaslms -vv grade -c prgi -a lab1 -u alice]]
\end{center}
The leading [[-vv]] really does set [[args.verbose]] to~2.
The [[grade]] subparser then resets it to [[False]], so what the user sees is
a command that accepted the flag and ignored it.
Nothing in the output suggests that a parser, rather than the command's own
logic, discarded the request.

We cannot lean on argparse to catch this for us.
Two options sharing a [[dest]] within \emph{one} parser raise
[[argparse.ArgumentError]]\,---\,that is the error
[[add_submission_options]] guards against when the same option might be
registered twice on one subparser.
A parser and its subparser, however, are separate objects, so the same
collision between them is perfectly legal and passes unremarked.
The invariant has to be maintained by hand instead: a subcommand that wants
verbosity or a delimiter simply reads [[args.verbose]] or [[args.delimiter]]
and lets the global option supply the value.

\subsubsection{Guarding the invariant}

A rule that argparse will not enforce, and whose violation leaves no trace at
run time, is exactly the kind of rule that decays.
So we check it.
We assemble the same parser tree that [[main]] assembles---by reusing the very
chunks [[main]] uses, so that a new global option or a newly registered
subcommand is covered the moment someone adds it---and then walk that tree
looking for a subparser that declares a global option's [[dest]].

Matching on [[dest]] rather than on how the flag is spelled is what makes the
test mean anything.
It is [[dest]] that names the namespace attribute, and the namespace attribute
is what gets overwritten.
A subcommand remains free to spend a short flag such as [[-f]] on its own
[[--file]] option: that lands in a different attribute and destroys nothing,
even though the top-level [[-f]] is spelled the same way.

<<test functions>>=
class TestGlobalOptionsAreNotShadowed:
    """Verify that no subcommand redeclares a global option"""

    <<shadowing test helpers>>
    <<shadowing test>>
@

We build the tree the way [[main]] does, with the global options on [[argp]]
and every subcommand's [[add_command]] on [[subp]]:
<<shadowing test helpers>>=
def build_parser(self):
    """Build the full parser tree, the way main() does."""
    argp = argparse.ArgumentParser()
    subp = argp.add_subparsers(title="commands", dest="command",
                               required=True)
    <<add global options to argp>>
    <<add subcommands to subp>>
    return argp

@

An action reaches the namespace---and can therefore clobber something---only
when it has a real destination and a default that argparse does not suppress.
The [[-h]] action fails the second condition, which is why every parser can
carry one without conflicting with its parent.
<<shadowing test helpers>>=
def declared_dests(self, parser):
    """Return the namespace attributes that parser itself fills in."""
    return {
        action.dest
        for action in parser._actions
        if action.dest is not argparse.SUPPRESS
        and action.default is not argparse.SUPPRESS
    }

@

Subcommands nest---[[submissions grade]], [[quizzes items list]]---so we
recurse, carrying the command path along to make a failure report readable.
<<shadowing test helpers>>=
def walk_subparsers(self, parser, path=""):
    """Yield (command path, parser) for every subparser, recursively."""
    for action in parser._actions:
        if not isinstance(action, argparse._SubParsersAction):
            continue
        for name, subparser in action.choices.items():
            subpath = f"{path} {name}".strip()
            yield subpath, subparser
            yield from self.walk_subparsers(subparser, subpath)

@

With those pieces the check itself is a single intersection per subparser:
<<shadowing test>>=
def test_no_subcommand_redeclares_a_global_option(self):
    """No subparser may declare a dest that a global option owns"""
    argp = self.build_parser()
    global_dests = self.declared_dests(argp)

    offenders = {}
    for path, subparser in self.walk_subparsers(argp):
        clashes = self.declared_dests(subparser) & global_dests
        if clashes:
            offenders[path] = sorted(clashes)

    assert not offenders, (
        "these subcommands redeclare global options, so their defaults "
        f"silently overwrite the global values: {offenders}")
@


\subsection{Logging in and setting up Canvas}

Each subcommand will have its own module in the package.
Each such module must have a function [[add_command]] that takes the [[subp]] 
parser as an argument and adds its command and options to that.
For example, the [[login]] command:
The \texttt{login} command is located in [[canvaslms.cli.login]].
<<modules>>=
import canvaslms.cli.login
<<add subcommands to subp>>=
canvaslms.cli.login.add_command(subp)
@

In short, each [[add_command]] must add a subparser 
([[subp.add_parser]]) that will set the [[func]] attribute.
Then we can execute the correct function and let that function check the
remaining arguments.
Not every command line reaches such a subparser, though, so we must be
prepared for a missing [[func]] before we run anything
(\cref{missing-subcommand}).

Each command function must take three arguments:
\begin{enumerate}
\item the configuration,
\item a Canvas object to use for interaction with Canvas,
\item the processed command-line arguments.
\end{enumerate}
This means that we must read the credentials to create the Canvas object.
One exception is the [[login]] command: this command doesn't need the Canvas 
object as it will be run before there are credentials.
<<run subcommands>>=
<<look up the command function as [[func]]>>

if func is None:
  <<report the missing subcommand>>

if (<<we don't need [[canvas]]>>):
  canvas = hostname = token = None
else:
  <<create canvas object>>

try:
  func(config, canvas, args)
except EmptyListError as e:
  if args.quiet == 0:
    err(1, str(e))
  else:
    sys.exit(1)
except MultipleMatchesError as e:
  if args.quiet == 0:
    err(1, str(e))
  else:
    sys.exit(1)
finally:
  <<save canvas to cache after command>>
@


\subsubsection{When no command function was set}%
\label{missing-subcommand}

The protocol above assumes that [[func]] is always set, but nothing in
argparse guarantees it.
A command that has subcommands of its own, like [[modules]], sets [[func]] on
each of \emph{its} subparsers: [[canvaslms modules list]] sets it, whereas
[[canvaslms modules]] on its own never reaches a subparser that would.
The resulting [[args]] then lacks the attribute altogether, so the very first
mention of [[args.func]]---the test for whether we need the Canvas object,
below---raises [[AttributeError]] and dumps a traceback on a user who merely
mistyped a command.

Each such command should of course require its subcommand (as [[modules]] now
does, \cref{modules-subcommand}), but the main program is where every command
ends up, so this is also where we can stop \emph{any} future subcommand from
crashing the same way.
We therefore ask for [[func]] in a way that tolerates its absence.
<<look up the command function as [[func]]>>=
func = getattr(args, "func", None)
@

The default matters more than it looks: the attribute is \emph{missing}, not
falsy, so no test of the form [[if args.func]] can help---such a test crashes
while evaluating its own condition.
And even when [[func]] is present, that test says nothing, since every
function object is truthy.
Only [[getattr]] with a default distinguishes the two cases.

Once [[func]] is [[None]] we know the user gave an incomplete command line, so
we do what argparse does for a missing required argument: show the help and
exit non-zero, letting the shell and any surrounding script see the failure.
We print to standard error, because standard output belongs to the CSV data
that pipelines consume (\cref{output-format}), and a failed run should not put
anything there.
The help we can print is the top-level one; the subparser that knows which
subcommands were available is local to the [[add_command]] call that created
it.
We therefore name the offending command from [[args.command]], which the
top-level subparser always sets, so the message points at the right place even
though the help is general.
<<report the missing subcommand>>=
argp.print_help(sys.stderr)
err(1, f"{args.command}: missing subcommand")
@

Let's verify that the lookup survives the [[Namespace]] shape that caused the
crash, namely one where argparse never set [[func]] at all:

<<test functions>>=
class TestCommandFunctionLookup:
    """Test how the CLI looks up the function to run"""

    def test_missing_func_gives_none(self):
        """
        A Namespace without func must yield None.

        This is what argparse produces for a command whose subcommand
        was omitted; reading args.func directly raises AttributeError.
        """
        args = argparse.Namespace(command="modules")

        # Use the same lookup as in the implementation
        <<look up the command function as [[func]]>>

        assert func is None

    def test_present_func_is_used(self):
        """A Namespace with func must yield that function"""
        def some_command(config, canvas, args):
            pass

        args = argparse.Namespace(command="courses", func=some_command)

        # Use the same lookup as in the implementation
        <<look up the command function as [[func]]>>

        assert func is some_command
@


\subsubsection{Which commands need the Canvas object}

We know that the [[login]] command doesn't need the Canvas object.
So we don't want to uncessarily create it (and fail) if the command is
[[login]] (that means the user is trying to set up Canvas).
(There will be more cases, but we'll return to them later.)
We compare against [[func]] rather than [[args.func]], for the reasons given
above; the same holds for every later addition to this chunk.
<<we don't need [[canvas]]>>=
func == canvaslms.cli.login.login_command
@

To create the Canvas object, we must read the credentials using the
shared cache loader.
That loader is responsible for reading credentials, normalizing the hostname,
restoring the encrypted cache when available, and creating a fresh
[[canvasapi.Canvas]] object otherwise.
By delegating here, the command-line interface and the top-level
[[canvaslms.canvas]] object both use the same configuration and retry policy.
<<create canvas object>>=
try:
  canvas, hostname, token = canvaslms.cli.cache.load_configured_canvas(
      config,
      use_cache=not args.no_cache,
  )
except canvaslms.cli.cache.CanvasCredentialsError as e:
  err(1, str(e))
@

After executing a command, we save the Canvas object to the cache for future
use.
We deliberately put this in a [[finally]] block (see the surrounding chunk),
so it runs whether the command succeeded, raised [[EmptyListError]] or
[[MultipleMatchesError]], or hit an unexpected exception.

The reason is that the cache state is monotonically improving: the decorators
in [[hacks/canvasapi.nw]] only \emph{add} or \emph{refresh} entries---never
corrupt or remove valid ones---so whatever new objects [[func]] managed
to fetch before an error are still worth persisting.
This matters in particular for long-running queries that filter a large
result set down to nothing: they raise [[EmptyListError]] only after warming
a substantial part of the cache, and saving preserves that warm-up.

We still skip saving when no Canvas object was used (e.g. the [[login]]
command) or when the user explicitly passed [[--no-cache]].

Note that [[hostname]] and [[token]] are only assigned real values together
with [[canvas]] in [[<<create canvas object>>]].
That is why the no-[[canvas]] branch above binds all three names to
[[None]]: this chunk runs in a [[finally]] block on \emph{every} path, so
leaving [[hostname]] and [[token]] unbound there would make the
[[if canvas]] guard the only thing standing between us and a
[[NameError]]---a trap for any future edit that touches the guard or reads
the names earlier.

<<save canvas to cache after command>>=
if canvas and not args.no_cache:
  canvaslms.cli.cache.save_canvas_cache(canvas, token, hostname)
@


