\paragraph{The \texttt{cache} command}

The \texttt{cache} command is located in [[canvaslms.cli.cache]].
<<modules>>=
import canvaslms.cli.cache
<<add subcommands to subp>>=
canvaslms.cli.cache.add_command(subp)
@

The [[cache]] command doesn't need a Canvas object, as it just manages the
cache files.
So we don't want to unnecessarily create the Canvas object in that case.
This holds for both of its subcommands, although for different reasons.
[[cache clear]] deletes the encrypted object cache, and it derives the file
name to delete from the configured hostname on its own, so it never touches
Canvas.
[[cache clear-attachments]] prunes stale files from the content-addressed
attachment store; that store is keyed by SHA-256 digests of the file contents
and is neither encrypted nor tied to any account, so the cleanup is a purely
local file operation.

Creating the Canvas object anyway would be worse than merely wasteful for
[[cache clear-attachments]].
Without stored credentials it aborts the command outright, even though nothing
it does requires them.
With credentials it decrypts the entire object cache on the way in and
re-encrypts it on the way out, so a command that only deletes stale files pays
the cost of rewriting a cache it never reads.
<<we don't need [[canvas]]>>=
or func == canvaslms.cli.cache.clear_command
or func == canvaslms.cli.cache.clear_attachments_command
